  "question": {
    "text": "<p>If the CFSE of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Ti</mi><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>96</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kJ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math>, this complex will absorb maximum at wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>nm</mi></math>. (nearest integer)</p><p>Assume Planck&#39; constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Js</mi></math>, Speed of light <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and Avogadro&#39;s constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>23</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "480",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Ti</mi><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>, oxidation state of Titanium is +3.<br />Thus, electronic configuration:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ti</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>&#8658;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></msub><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>We know&nbsp;CFSE&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mfenced><mi>&#916;o</mi></math>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;(&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;o</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Octahedral</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>splitting</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>energy</mi></math>)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;o</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>96</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>23</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math></p><p>Using planck&#39;s equation and fact that octahedral splitting energy per complex is:<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#916;o</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>hc</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>480</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>480</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math></p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
